  "panels": [
    {
      "id": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "title": "Reading this dashboard",
      "gridPos": {
        "x": 0,
        "y": 0,
        "w": 24,
        "h": 14
      },
      "options": {
        "mode": "markdown",
        "content": "# The breaker experiment \u2014 \u00a7XIII\n\n**A-B-B-A at 1,600 rps offered, 3-minute arms, Lambda reserved concurrency 900.** Red = control (`/db`, unguarded), green = treatment (`/db-breaker`, resilience4j circuit breaker).\n\n**\u00a7IX predicted** a deliberate shedder would raise goodput *and* cut cost at identical offered load past the knee.\n**Cost: confirmed** \u2014 GB-s per successful request 2.593 \u2192 1.092, and the two treatments' replicate ranges do not overlap.\n**Goodput: not established** \u2014 1.26\u00d7 mean, but ranges overlap (B2 355.0/s is worse than A2 411.1/s). A single A1-vs-B1 pair showed 1.90\u00d7 and would have supported a claim the full ordering dissolves.\n\n**The two bar panels are the verdict.** Read them first: cost separates cleanly by treatment, goodput does not. Everything below them is mechanism.\n\n**Reading the time series.** Lines break where no data exists \u2014 Lambda publishes metrics only while invoked, so the long empty stretches are the recovery gates between arms, which were genuinely idle. They are gaps, not zeros and not declines. The gate after the control arm needed 3 probes and ~8 minutes to clear; each breaker arm needed 1.\n\n\u26a0 **The billed-duration distribution panel is absent by necessity.** It read from Logs Insights over `/aws/lambda/lra-database-resilience`, deleted with the stack at teardown. Its percentiles (control p50 199 ms / p95 13,529 ms vs breaker p50 23 ms / p95 397 ms) survive in `loadtest/results/`. CloudWatch *metrics* persist and are unaffected \u2014 every panel here resolves against deleted resources."
      }
    },
